6.11.text:
Article 20

(1) The Regional Government shall protect the fulfillment of the rights of child workers in the informal sector.

(2) Child Workers in the informal sector as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. domestic workers;
b. shoe shiners;
c. street vendors;
d. scavengers, buskers;
e. parking attendants;
f. construction laborers; and
g. other informal sector jobs that employ children. (3) Every person who employs children in the formal sector must observe the requirements:
a. minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the category of worst forms of child labor minimum 18 (eighteen) years;
b. obtaining written consent from parents/guardians of child workers; c. having a written employment agreement between the employer and the parents/guardians of child workers and obtaining endorsement from the competent authority; d. not employed at night;
e. working time of a maximum of 3 (three) hours per day;
f. not employed in places or environments that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;
g. providing opportunities to obtain education according to their talents and interests;
h. employing for light types of work; and
i. providing one day off per week.

